## When to Use Redis vs Database

| Use Case | Redis | Database |
|----------|----|----------|
| Session storage | Yes — fast, TTL built-in | Can work, slower |
| Page/query cache | Yes — perfect | No — this is what caching avoids |
| Rate limiting counters | Yes — atomic INCR | Risky — race conditions |
| Distributed lock | Yes — via SET NX PX | Complex — advisory locks |
| Leaderboard | Yes — sorted sets | Possible, complex |
| Durable primary data | No (unless persistence configured) | Yes |
| Complex queries/joins | No | Yes |

## Spring Boot Setup

```yaml
spring:
  data:
    redis:
      host: ${REDIS_HOST:localhost}
      port: 6379
      password: ${REDIS_PASSWORD:}
      lettuce:  # default client
        pool:
          max-active: 8
          max-idle: 8
          min-idle: 0
          max-wait: -1ms
```

